<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#biblical-hebrew">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biblical Hebrew</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Classical Hebrew</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, also called Classical Hebrew, is an archaic form of the Hebrew language, of the Canaanitic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, spoken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> in the Land of Israel. It is attested in inscriptions from about the 10th century BCE, when it was almost identical to Phoenician, and persisted as a spoken first language through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, which ended in 70 CE, before developing into Mishnaic Hebrew. It was first written in the Paleo-Hebrew alphabet, displaced after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-aramaic">Imperial Aramaic</a> alphabet, and its vowel marks, the Tiberian vocalization, were added by the Masoretes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biblical-hebrew.jpg" alt="Biblical Hebrew" width="330" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tamar Hayardeni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiloach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> developed in the latter half of the second millennium BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, between the Jordan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. In its earliest stages it was not highly differentiated from Ugaritic and the Canaanite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, and the Northwest Semitic languages formed a dialect continuum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, with Phoenician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> at either extreme. Hebrew belongs with Phoenician in the Canaanite subgroup, which also includes Ammonite, Edomite and Moabite. The oldest layer, Archaic Hebrew, appears in the poetry of the Bible, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-deborah">Song of Deborah</a>, and in inscriptions of about 1000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> has the typically Semitic system of roots, usually of three consonants, arranged in patterns to form words. It distinguishes masculine and feminine genders and singular, plural and the uncommon dual numbers. Verbs have two main conjugations, the suffix and the prefix forms, whose meaning for tense and aspect is debated and depends much on context. The usual word order is verb, subject, object. The language of the Bible had other names: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> calls it the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> or Judean, and the term Hebrew comes from Greek and Mishnaic Hebrew texts, its origin obscure.</p><p>The consonantal text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> reflects several stages of the language, with evidence of regional dialects, including differences between the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> and the southern Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. In the biblical story of Jephthah, his forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a> identified fleeing Ephraimites by their pronunciation of the word shibboleth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Spoken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> persisted as a first language through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period and eventually developed into Mishnaic Hebrew, which was used as a second language until the 5th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> kept the Paleo-Hebrew script, and still use a descendant of it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-aramaic">Imperial Aramaic</a> alphabet gradually displaced it and became the source of the present Hebrew alphabet. The early writing systems marked consonants only, and certain letters called matres lectionis came increasingly to mark vowels.</p><p>The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> writing yet found, an ink text of five lines on a potsherd from Khirbet Qeiyafa, dates to the 10th century BCE. The consonantal text of the Bible was transmitted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> and revised during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period. The Masoretes added the Tiberian vowel marks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, after 600 CE, and this is the system still widely used.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biblical Hebrew” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Biblical Hebrew</b> had a distinctive verbal system, in which narratives were told in a form combining the conjunction “and” with a verb form, and its usual word order placed the verb before the subject.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Phoenician is a Canaanite language of the Northwest Semitic division of the Semitic family, similar to and mutually intelligible with <b>Biblical Hebrew</b> and the other languages of its subgroup.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Apparent Tamil loanwords in <b>Biblical Hebrew</b> from before 500 BCE are the oldest attestation of all.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
